{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And I will give grass in your field.</b> He attributed the giving of grass to Himself, may He be exalted, against the opinions of those who think that the world was delivered over to the supernal beings and to the forces that cause growth. Therefore the Torah came to heal the sickness of their belief, to make known that He, may He be exalted, is the One who causes growth and gives grass, in the manner of what is said (Psalms 104:14): ״מצמיח חציר לבהמה״ — 'who causes grass to sprout for the beast.' Therefore He said, ונתתי עשב — 'and I will give grass' — in the same language as He said, ונתתי מטר ארצכם — 'and I will give rain for your land' — for just as He is the One who sends rain, so is He the One who gives grass; and one verse encompassed both of them, namely what is written (Zechariah 10:1): ומטר גשם יתן להם לאיש עשב בשדה — 'and the rain of showers He will give them, to each one grass in the field.'" "<b>And you shall eat and be satisfied.</b> It refers back to your grain, your wine, and your oil. Or its meaning may be: 'and I will give grass in your field for your cattle, and you shall eat and be satisfied' — when the grass is abundant in the field and the pasture is good, then 'and you shall eat and be satisfied,' for this is a sign of satiety in the sustenance of human beings. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: ונתתי עשב בשדך לבהמתך — 'and I will give grass in your field for your cattle' — והדר ואכלת — 'and afterward, and you shall eat' — from here it is forbidden for a person to eat until he has given food to his animal."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:47:32.532847289Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}